# netsurf: Resource Timing -- each fetch a PerformanceResourceTiming entry
# on the page's timeline (patch 282)
# Generated 2026-10-13
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 281)
#
# performance.getEntriesByType('resource') was always empty: nothing
# recorded a fetch. Wikimedia's network probe (ext.wikimediaEvents
# networkprobe, run on a sampled page view) fetches each measure-*.wikimedia
# .org target and reads its entry back with getEntriesByName(url): with
# none, extractProbeData read a field of undefined, the probe's promise
# rejected with undefined, and the scorecard logged Wikipedia's "unhandled
# rejection: undefined". hCaptcha's hsw and other fingerprint scripts walk
# the resource entries too.
#
#  - the engine: the html handler registers each subresource fetch it
#    starts with the QuickJS bridge (macsurf_rt_begin: <link> stylesheets
#    "link", scripts "script", images and replaced src "img", CSS
#    backgrounds "css", detached images -- new Image(), images outside the
#    tree -- "img"); hlcache reports DONE / ERROR and each handle's release
#    (macsurf_rt_event, weak no-ops in macsurf_frontend_stubs.c for builds
#    without the bridge). A finished fetch is an entry kept per document --
#    URL, initiator, start and end on the engine's clock, body size, HTTP
#    status, Content-Type, Timing-Allow-Origin -- until the document's
#    realm reads its timeline
#    (__vitaResourceTake: times from the realm's time origin, in 100 us
#    steps as performance.now). A URL is one entry per document, as a
#    browser's memory cache makes it: the engine fetches a stylesheet
#    twice (it is discovered again) and rebuilt boxes' images again, from
#    its cache. Entries die with their document (macsurf_js_notify_content
#    _freed); at most 250 per document, 1024 in all.
#  - vita_polyfill.js: the entries are PerformanceResourceTiming objects
#    (patch 270's interface, which had only the navigation entry) with the
#    Level 2/3 attributes and toJSON, merged into getEntries,
#    getEntriesByType('resource') and getEntriesByName (startTime order);
#    fetch() and XMLHttpRequest are recorded here ("fetch" when the
#    response arrives, "xmlhttprequest" at loadend); clearResourceTimings /
#    setResourceTimingBufferSize work on the buffer (250, as browsers;
#    resourcetimingbufferfull fires when it is full). What the engine does
#    not measure reads as a browser reports a reused connection: the
#    network phases equal fetchStart, the response starts when it ends
#    (subresources) or when headers arrive (fetch), the sizes are the body
#    as received (transferSize + 300 for the headers, as Chromium). A
#    cross-origin entry without a Timing-Allow-Origin naming this origin
#    shows only startTime / fetchStart / responseEnd / duration -- phases,
#    sizes, nextHopProtocol 0 or "" -- and one neither same-origin nor
#    CORS (an image or script from elsewhere) no responseStatus or
#    contentType, as the specification and Chromium (checked field by
#    field on a localhost-vs-127.0.0.1 image). The polyfill runs again when the real document arrives
#    and that pass defines fetch anew, so the hooks are put back on each
#    pass (the buffer is kept from the first).
#
# Not recorded: @import-ed stylesheets and fonts (fetched by the CSS
# handler, not the document), iframes' own documents, navigations;
# PerformanceObserver still delivers nothing for 'resource'.
#
# hCaptcha: hsw's request field pem, {} before (no entries), now carries
# its api.hcaptcha.com entry as Chromium's does, in Chromium's shape
# ({csc, csch, cscrt: 0, cscft}: cross-origin, so requestStart reads 0).
# The demo still refuses (797 bytes); the hsw replay is identical.
#
# Wikimedia's own Probenet class, run against the gate server (scratch, not
# committed): 6 pulses, every one with duration, sizes, status and pulse id,
# no errors -- the same as Chromium; before, it rejected on the first pulse.
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-resource-timing.html (RESTIMING 9/9, Chromium
# 9/9; the build before passes 0).
